# Architecture and File Placement (facturador-crecer-api)

Follow hexagonal layout. Place new files next to similar existing ones. Do not invent new top-level folders.

## Layers

| Kind | Path |
|---|---|
| Use cases | `src/application/useCases/{UseCaseName}/index.ts` |
| Entities | `src/domain/entities/{Name}.entity.ts` |
| Repository contracts | `src/domain/repositories/{Name}.repository.ts` |
| Domain services | `src/domain/services/{feature}/{Name}.service.ts` |
| Shared helpers | `src/domain/services/utils/` (only if reused) |
| Mappers | `src/domain/mappers/{feature}/` |
| Exceptions | `src/domain/exceptions/{common\|entity\|user}/` |
| Controllers | `src/infrastructure/driving-adapters/API/controllers/{feature}/` |
| Routes | `src/infrastructure/driving-adapters/API/routes/` |
| Middlewares | `src/infrastructure/driving-adapters/API/middlewares/` |
| DynamoDB repos | `src/infrastructure/implementations/AWS/dynamoDB/` |

## Flow

Route → Controller → Use Case → Domain service / repository interface → Infrastructure implementation.

## Rules

- Controllers stay thin: validate input, call use case, map HTTP response.
- Business rules live in use cases or domain services, never in routes.
- Reuse existing mappers/services before creating new ones.
- Prefer editing an existing file over adding a new abstraction.
- Match existing naming: `createReceipt.controller.ts`, `ReceiptCreator`, `DynamoDBReceiptRepository.ts`.
